.docname {Drawing and stamps}
.include {docs}

You edit the board with three tools, chosen at the top of the pattern palette: **Draw**, **Erase**, and **Stamp**.

## Draw and erase

With **Draw** selected, drag with the left mouse button to bring cells to life. With **Erase** selected, the same drag kills cells. The right mouse button always erases, whichever tool is selected.

Cells you drag across are changed one at a time, but the whole drag counts as a single edit for [undo](#undo-and-redo).

## Stamps

A stamp places a whole pattern with one click.

1. Select a pattern: click its thumbnail in the palette.
2. Move the mouse over the board. A blue *ghost*, outlined with the stamp's footprint, shows exactly which cells will be placed. It has its own colour so it never passes for cells already on the board.
3. Click to place the pattern. The stamp stays selected, so you can keep clicking to place copies.

.keybinding {C} drops the stamp and goes back to the Draw tool. Clicking **Stamp** with no pattern selected picks the first one in the library. Switching to Draw or Erase hides the ghost but keeps the stamp selected, so clicking **Stamp** again brings it back.

You can add your own patterns, either for the current session through [RLE import](patterns.qd#importing-a-pattern) or permanently as [built-in patterns](patterns.qd#built-in-patterns).

### Rotating and flipping

| Key               | Button     | Effect                                   |
|-------------------|------------|------------------------------------------|
| .keybinding {Z}   | **Rotate** | Rotate the stamp a quarter turn clockwise |
| .keybinding {F}   | **Flip**   | Mirror the stamp horizontally            |

The stamp preview in the palette and the ghost on the board both show the orientation that will be placed. The Rotate and Flip buttons are disabled unless the Stamp tool is active.

## Board actions

| Action        | Key               | Effect                                                     |
|---------------|-------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Randomize** | .keybinding {R}   | Replace the board using the density and seed chosen in Settings |
| **Clear**     | .keybinding {X}   | Kill every cell                                            |

Both are edits, so you can undo them. Randomize keeps the rule, edges, and generation
counter. In Settings, **Randomization** lets you choose a whole-number density from
0–100% (25% by default), and an optional seed. Leave the seed blank for a fresh board
each time; the same seed, density, and dimensions repeat the same cell layout.
**Apply randomization** sets the preferences; **Randomize now** also replaces the board.
Density is a probability for each cell, so the population is approximate. At 0% the
board is empty; at 100% every cell is alive.

## Undo and redo

| Key                                              | Effect                         |
|--------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|
| .keybinding {U} or .keybinding {Mod+Z}           | Undo the last edit and pause   |
| .keybinding {Mod+Shift+Z}                        | Redo the last undone edit      |

The toolbar has **Undo** and **Redo** buttons as well, shown as arrows curving left and right.

These are edits, and can be undone:

- drawing and erasing (one edit per drag)
- placing a stamp
- randomizing and clearing the board
- changing the rule, including an import that changes the rule

Undo restores the board, the generation counter, and the rule as they were before the edit, and pauses playback so you can see the result. Redo reapplies an undone edit and also leaves playback paused. Making a new edit discards anything left to redo.

### History limits

History keeps up to 32 edits, within a budget of about 16 MiB of board snapshots. On very large boards fewer edits fit, but at least one is always kept.

Resizing the board clears the undo and redo history, because old snapshots no longer fit the new size. Resizing the *window* does not affect history, unless [Fill canvas](settings.qd#fill-canvas) is on and the board resizes with it.

## Save and reopen workspaces

Open **Import / Export**, then use **Workspace files**. **Save workspace…** opens the
system file dialog in your Documents folder, or wherever you last saved or opened a
workspace. The dialog asks before replacing an existing file.

**Open workspace…** lets you choose a `.golw` file and checks it. If it is valid, you're
asked whether to replace the current board and imported pattern library. **Cancel**
(or Escape) keeps your current work. Files preserve board dimensions, exact cell positions, generation,
rule, boundary mode, playback speed, randomization density and seed, and imported pattern names, cells, and rules.
Replacing pauses playback, clears undo/redo, and turns Fill canvas off. Invalid files
are reported without asking, and leave the current workspace intact.

Files are versioned JSON containing RLE data. Limits are 32 MiB per file, 256 imported
patterns, 1024 bytes per pattern name, and 4,194,304 live cells across the imported
patterns. The board doesn't count toward the cell limit. Camera position,
active stamp, cell-age trails, and undo history are not saved. Display settings remain
in the separate settings file. Saving is explicit; there is no automatic recovery.
